APP.PY : This script is a foundational Python Flask API. While simple, it illustrates a standard microservice architecture. From a DevOps perspective, it establishes an 
explicit listening configuration on port 5000 across all interfaces via 0.0.0.0, making it fully ready to receive routed traffic inside a Docker container or an AWS EC2 
instance. The JSON response it generates acts as a reliable health check endpoint for monitoring tools and cloud load balancers.
a. from flask import Flask, jsonify
Flask is the core class used to initialize the web application framework.
jsonify is a helper function that converts Python dictionaries or variables into a JSON-formatted response with the correct HTTP header.
b. app = Flask(__name__)
This creates an instance of the Flask class. The __name__ argument tells Flask where to look for resources like templates and static files. In single-module applications 
like this, it acts as a unique identifier for the app package.
@app.route('/')
def home():
    return jsonify(status="healthy", message="Flask Devops App Operational")
@app.route('/') is a Python decorator that binds the root URL path (/) to the home() function. When a user or load balancer hits the server's IP at the root level, 
this function triggers.
The home() function returns a JSON payload indicating that the application is operational.
if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)
if __name__ == '__main__': ensures that the development server only boots up if the script is run directly (e.g., python app.py) rather than imported as a module by 
another script.
app.run(...) starts the built-in development web server.
host='0.0.0.0': This tells the Flask framework to bind to all available network interfaces on the host operating system. If it were left as default (127.0.0.1), the app 
would only accept loopback traffic from inside its own local system. Binding to 0.0.0.0 allows outside entities (such as your host computer accessing a Docker container, 
or an external AWS target group) to communicate with the app. 
port=5000: This instructs the application process to listen on TCP port 5000.

Terraform explaination :
The infrastructure is built within a secure Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) featuring two public and two private subnets distributed across two Availability Zones for high 
availability. A Docker host and SonarQube for code quality assurance are provisioned on an EC2 instance within the public subnet, while EKS worker nodes reside in private 
subnets for enhanced security with outbound traffic routed through a NAT Gateway.

Question 1: "Why did you place the EKS worker nodes in the private subnet, and how do they pull Docker images or updates if they don’t have a public IP?"
I placed the EKS worker nodes in the private subnet for strict security, ensuring they are completely hidden from direct internet threats. To allow them to pull Docker 
images or install system updates, I deployed a NAT Gateway in the public subnet. The private route table is configured to route all outbound traffic through this NAT Gateway,
allowing safe one-way communication to the internet while blocking any outside entity from initiating a connection directly to our worker nodes.

Question 2: "Your Security Group opens ports 22, 5000, and 9000 to 0.0.0.0/0 (the entire internet). In a real production environment, how would you make this more secure?"
To harden this setup for production, I would restrict 0.0.0.0/0 access. For Port 22 (SSH), I would only allow our company’s corporate VPN IP or use AWS Systems Manager 
Session Manager instead of exposing SSH to the world. For Port 9000 (SonarQube), I would limit access to our specific developer team IP range or place it behind an internal 
Load Balancer. For Port 5000, instead of exposing the port directly on the host, I would route traffic to it strictly through an AWS Application Load Balancer.

Question 3: "You mentioned using 2 public and 2 private subnets across 2 different Availability Zones. What happens to your EKS applications if one AWS Availability Zone 
goes down completely?"
Because I built this with high availability in mind, if one Availability Zone experiences an outage, our application will remain operational. Amazon EKS will automatically 
detect that the nodes in the affected zone are unhealthy. Since the EKS Node Group spans across two zones, Kubernetes will immediately spin up replacement microservice 
containers on the surviving worker nodes in the second, healthy Availability Zone, ensuring zero or minimal downtime for our users.

DockerFile explaination :
In this Dockerfile, I focus on two main things: keeping the image lightweight and making the build fast using Docker caching. 
First, I used python:3.11-slim as the base image instead of the full Python image to keep our container size small and secure. 
Second, I set the working directory to /app and copied only the requirements.txt file  I ran pip install with --no-cache-dir to install dependencies without saving temporary 
junk files.
Third, I copied the rest of the application code. By doing this after installing dependencies, I leverage Docker layer caching. If our code changes but dependencies stay 
the same, Docker skips the slow installation step, making our CI/CD builds super fast. 
Finally, I exposed port 5000 for network communication and used the CMD instruction to run the Python application when the container starts.

Question 1: "You have EXPOSE 5000 in your Dockerfile. Does this automatically open port 5000 to the public internet or your VPC network?"
No, EXPOSE 5000 does not open the port to the public internet or the VPC. It is mostly documentational. It tells Docker and other engineers which port the application 
intends to listen on inside the container. To actually make it accessible outside the host, I still need to map the port when running the container (using -p 5000:5000) and 
ensure my AWS Security Groups allow traffic on that port.

Question 2: "When you build this Docker image using a CI/CD pipeline, where do you store it so that your EKS worker nodes in the private subnet can pull and run it?"
"I push the built Docker image to a repository on Docker Hub. Since my EKS worker nodes are sitting in a private subnet with no public IP, they route their outbound traffic 
through our NAT Gateway to reach the public internet and pull the image from Docker Hub. If the repository is private, I configure a Kubernetes ImagePullSecret containing 
my Docker Hub credentials so the worker nodes can authenticate and pull the image securely."

Question 3: "If your Python Flask app inside this container needs to connect to a database hosted elsewhere in your VPC,what network configuration determines if it can reach it?"
The connection depends on two things: AWS Security Groups and Routing Tables. First, the database’s Security Group must have an inbound rule allowing traffic from the EKS 
worker nodes' Security Group on the database port. Second, since both are inside my secure VPC, the internal route tables will automatically handle the private IP routing 
between the subnets, keeping the database traffic completely private and secure."

Docker-Compose.yml explaination :
"This Docker Compose file sets up a complete, self-hosted code quality platform. Instead of using SonarQube's default temporary database, I paired it with a dedicated 
PostgreSQL database service for data persistence.Inside the file, I defined two services: sonarqube (the web interface/application engine) and db (the database backend).
To ensure data is never lost when containers restart or upgrade, I configured Docker Volumes to safely store SonarQube's configuration, logs, and database tables on the 
host machine.
From a networking perspective, I isolated both containers inside a private, custom bridge network called sonar-net. This allows them to communicate securely using their 
service names. Finally, I exposed port 9000 on the host so our development team or CI/CD pipelines can access the SonarQube web dashboard."

Question 1: "Look at the SONAR_JDBC_URL. You didn't use an IP address; you used jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/sonar. How does SonarQube resolve the word 'db' to reach the 
database?"
"Because both services are attached to the same custom bridge network (sonar-net), Docker's built-in embedded DNS server automatically handles name resolution. It resolves 
the service name db directly to the private container IP assigned to the PostgreSQL database. This acts just like a local DNS server in a traditional network enterprise."

Question 2: "Why is port 9000 mapped under the sonarqube service, but the database service (db) has no ports mapped at all?"
"We only map port 9000 for SonarQube because users and CI/CD tools need to access the web dashboard from outside the Docker host. The database, however, does not need to be 
exposed to the public internet or external networks. Since both containers share the internal sonar-net network, SonarQube can talk to Postgres on port 5432 completely 
internally, keeping our database isolated and secure."

Question 3: "What is the purpose of the depends_on flag under the sonarqube service?"
"depends_on controls the startup order of the containers. It tells Docker Compose that the database container must be created and started before the SonarQube container boots
up. This prevents SonarQube from crashing on startup due to the database connection not being available yet."

.github/workflow/ci-cd.yml file explaination :
designed and implemented an automated end-to-end GitOps CI/CD pipeline using GitHub Actions for a Python Flask application. It triggers automatically when changes hit the 
application folder. The pipeline dynamically provisions an AWS environment using Terraform, validates and runs Python unit tests, builds a Docker container, pushes it to 
Docker Hub, updates our GitOps Kubernetes manifests, and finally deploys the application to an Amazon EKS cluster.

[Push to app/**] 
       ▼
 1. provision-infra  (Terraform Apply on AWS + dynamic SonarQube IP export)
       │
       ▼
 2. test-and-scan    (Python Pytest unit checks)
       │
       ▼
 3. build-and-push   (Docker Image Build -> Tagged with Commit SHA -> Registry)
       │
       ▼
 4. update-manifest  (GitOps: Automated 'sed' updates k8s deployment tag)
       │
       ▼
 5. deploy-to-eks    (AWS Kubeconfig update -> kubectl apply to EKS)

Q: Why did you configure permissions: id-token: write at the root of the workflow?
Answer: "This is a security best practice for cloud authentication. It enables OIDC(OpenID Connect). Instead of storing long-lived, high-risk AWS Access Keys inside GitHub 
Secrets, GitHub Actions exchanges a short-lived OIDC token with AWS IAM. AWS validates the token identity and issues temporary security credentials that expire automatically
when the job finishes."

Q: In deploy-to-eks, how does GitHub runner actually gain authority to communicate with your EKS cluster?Answer: "First, the runner uses AWS credentials to run aws eks 
update-kubeconfig, which generates a local configuration file containing the cluster's endpoint and access tokens. Then, when kubectl apply runs, it uses that kubeconfig 
context to authenticate against the EKS control plane. For this to succeed, the IAM role assumed by the runner must be mapped inside the EKS cluster's authentication system 
(like aws-auth ConfigMap or EKS Access Entries)."

Q: I noticed an automated step that runs terraform fmt and then pushes code back to the repository. Why did you design it this way, and what risk does it introduce?
Answer:
The Benefit: "It enforces strict stylistic consistency across the team without relying on developers to remember to format code locally.
"The Risk & Solution: "The risk is causing an infinite pipeline loop. If a bot commits code, it could trigger the pipeline again. I mitigated this by adding [skip ci] to 
the commit message, telling GitHub Actions to ignore this specific automated commit."

Q: How are you managing the Terraform State file (.tfstate) in this pipeline?
Answer: "In a team pipeline, state files must never be stored locally or pushed to Git—which is why my workflow explicitly appends .tfstate to the .gitignore during the 
format step. In production, I configure a remote backend (like an AWS S3 bucket with DynamoDB state locking) inside the terraform block. This ensures that when the runner 
executes terraform init, it safely pulls the shared, locked state from the cloud."

Q: Your pipeline has a job called update-manifest. Why update a YAML file in Git instead of just running kubectl set image directly on the cluster?Answer: "This is the core 
principle of GitOps. Your Git repository must always be the single source of truth for the cluster's actual state. If I used kubectl set image, the cluster would change, 
but the code in Git would remain outdated. By using sed to update k8s/deployment.yml and committing it back to Git, we maintain an immutable audit trail. Anyone looking at 
the repository knows exactly what container version is live."

Q: Why did you pass ${{ github.sha }} to your Docker tags instead of using a tag like :latest or a semantic version (e.g., :v1.0.0)?Answer: ":latest is a massive anti-pattern
in production because it is mutable—you never truly know what code it points to, making rollbacks impossible. Semantic versioning is great for releases, but for continuous 
deployment, using the Git Commit SHA ensures absolute immutability. Every single built image links perfectly back to the exact line of code that triggered it."

Q: How does the test-and-scan job get the URL for SonarQube if the infrastructure is built dynamically in the previous job?Answer: "I leveraged GitHub Actions Job Outputs. 
In the provision-infra job, after running terraform apply, I use a step with an ID to capture the raw Terraform output variable (sonarqube_server_ip). I append that value 
to $GITHUB_OUTPUT. The job itself maps that step output to a job-level output called sonar_url. Because test-and-scan specifies needs: provision-infra, it can securely read 
that dynamic URL context using ${{ needs.provision-infra.outputs.sonar_url }}."

4. Architectural Critique
Q: If you could improve or optimize this workflow right now, what changes would you make?Tip: Interviewers love this because it proves you see the flaws in your own work and 
know how to progress.
Answer: "There are three optimizations I would implement next 
Add Security Scanning for IaC: I would introduce a step using trivy or checkov right after terraform validate to scan my Terraform files for security misconfigurations 
before running an apply. 
Introduce Caching: The Python dependency steps download packages from scratch every single run. I would implement actions/cache for the pip directory to slice off runner 
minutes.
Decouple the Destroy Job: Having a destroy-infra job in a main deployment workflow is dangerous. While it requires manual triggering, a simple human misconfiguration could 
trigger a production teardown. I would isolate infrastructure destruction into a completely separate, restricted workflow file."


